ing to a bad request. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user doesn\'t have the correct Prometheus query syntax. So, suggesting a simpler query first, like `http_requests_total` without the status filter, could help. If that works, then they can add the status filter. \n\nBut the user\'s main goal is to find faults, so checking the available metrics first would be more efficient. Let me check the available tools again. The `get_metrics` tool allows any PromQL query. So, the next step should be to query for available metrics related to HTTP requests to confirm the correct metric name and structure. \n\nTherefore, the best tool to use now is `get_metrics` with a query like `http_*` to list all HTTP metrics. This will help identify the correct metric name and ensure the syntax is right. Once the correct metric is known, the user can then apply filters for non-200 status codes.\n</think>\n\nget_metrics  \nThe previous PromQL query had a syntax error due to missing braces. To safely query metrics, I\'ll first retrieve available HTTP-related metrics using a general pattern like `http_*` to identify the correct metric name and structure. This avoids errors and provides insight into potential error tracking metrics (e.g., `http_requests_total` with status codes). \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "get_metrics", "arguments": {"query": "http_*"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 582, 'prompt_tokens': 3396, 'total_tokens': 3978, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfeb0-4976-7c00-87f0-b41e047712ed-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3396, 'output_tokens': 582, 'total_tokens': 3978}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', addit